 "edits": [
  {
   "op": "replace_brief",
   "brief": "nursemaid",
   "html": "<div class=\"brief sp\" id=\"nursemaid\" data-entry=\"hub\" data-lens=\"peds\" data-spine=\"2\" data-shelf=\"fm peds\" data-bp=\"msk\">\n<h4>Arm Injury in a Young Child</h4>\n<p class=\"sub\">pull or fall &middot; reduce or radiograph &middot; after reduction &middot; supracondylar nerves and artery &middot; suspecting abuse &middot; treatment by cause</p>\n<nav class=\"sp-step\" aria-label=\"Decision spine\"><a href=\"#na-s1\"><b>1</b><span>Presentation</span></a><a href=\"#na-s2\"><b>2</b><span>Urgent</span></a><a href=\"#na-dx\"><b>3</b><span>Differential</span></a><a href=\"#na-s4\"><b>4</b><span>Workup</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>5</b><span>Criteria</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>6</b><span>Severity</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>7</b><span>Treatment</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>8</b><span>Follow-up</span></a><a href=\"#nm-bank\" class=\"sp-prac\"><span>Practice</span></a></nav>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"na-s1\"><b class=\"sp-n\">1</b>Presentation</h5>\n<div class=\"dp\"><span class=\"lbl\">Bottom line</span><ul class=\"bl\"><li>A <b>pull</b> on the arm, a child <b>under 5</b> holding the forearm <b>pronated</b>, and <b>no swelling, deformity or bony tenderness</b>: <b>radial head subluxation</b> (nursemaid&#x27;s elbow); <b>reduce</b> without a radiograph</li><li>A <b>fall</b> on an outstretched hand with a <b>swollen elbow</b>: <b>radiographs first</b>; a <b>posterior fat pad</b> is an occult fracture</li><li>A story that <b>does not fit</b>, or a long-bone fracture <b>before walking</b>: suspect <b>abuse</b>; <b>report to child protective services</b> and a <b>skeletal survey</b> under 2 years</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"sp-script sp-s2\"><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">What it is</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>A young child who will not move or use one arm</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Sort by</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>The mechanism (a pull or a fall), then the elbow exam, then age <span class=\"op op-p\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"and\"></span> the reduction maneuver both diagnoses and treats</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Hands off</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>Suspected abuse has its own brief<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"limp\">The Limping Child</button></div></p></div></div></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"na-s2\"><b class=\"sp-n\">2</b>Urgent risks</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-rows\">\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Brachial artery injury</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Pale, cool or pulseless hand: a supracondylar fracture may injure the brachial artery</b><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the hand is pale or pulseless, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">urgent orthopedics; splint the elbow as it lies, no reduction in the emergency department</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Compartment syndrome</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Tense, painful forearm after an elbow fracture; untreated, Volkmann ischemic contracture, a clawlike hand</b><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the forearm is tense and painful, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">fasciotomy without delay</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Abuse</span><b class=\"sp-f\">A long-bone fracture before walking, a delay in care, or a story that does not fit the injury <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"na-pop-4\">How often</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"na-pop-4\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Humerus fractures and abuse</span>Abuse causes up to 54% of humerus fractures under age 3; a displaced physeal fracture of the humerus is 13 times as likely as a displaced supracondylar fracture to be abuse</span></b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> abuse is suspected, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">report to child protective services; skeletal survey under 2 years</span>.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"na-dx\"><b class=\"sp-n\">3</b>Differential</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Arm injury in a young child, most tempting first</caption><thead><tr><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Fingerprint</th><th>Decides it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Radial head subluxation <span class=\"aka\">(nursemaid&#x27;s elbow)</span> <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"na-pop-1\">Why under 5</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"na-pop-1\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Mechanism</span>At 2 to 3 years the head of the radius is only as wide as its neck, so a pull slides the annular ligament over it and into the radiocapitellar joint</span></td><td><span>Under 5, most often 2 to 3; lifted or pulled by the hand or wrist</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Minutes</b>: uses the arm within about 30 minutes of reduction; elbow slightly flexed, forearm pronated, no swelling or bony tenderness <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"na-pop-2\">Which nerve</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"na-pop-2\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Radial head subluxation and the radial nerve</span>The deep (motor) branch of the radial nerve is pinched, so the forearm extensors are weak; the wrist still extends and the back of the hand keeps its feeling</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Supracondylar humerus fracture</td><td><span>Ages 3 to 11; usually a fall onto an outstretched hand that hyperextends the elbow</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Swollen, painful elbow with limited motion; posterior fat pad on the lateral film <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"na-pop-3\">Fat pads</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"na-pop-3\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Elbow radiograph</span>A posterior fat pad on a true lateral film is always abnormal: specific for an effusion, though not sensitive. A posterior fat pad, or a large anterior one (sail sign), is treated as an occult fracture</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Abuse <span class=\"aka\">(non-accidental trauma)</span><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button></div></td><td><span>Not yet walking; a delay in care, or a history that does not fit</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Spiral long-bone or rib fractures, or fractures of different ages</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta</td><td><span>Repeated fractures with minimal trauma; mimics abuse with bruising</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Blue sclerae, short stature</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Midshaft humerus fracture</td><td><span>Wrist drop: the radial nerve in the spiral groove</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Wrist drop; radial head subluxation spares wrist extension and the back of the hand</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Buckle fracture of the proximal radius</td><td><span>Older child who usually points to the back of the upper forearm</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Point tenderness over bone: radiograph before any maneuver</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"lcw\"><div class=\"ttl\"><span>Age at onset</span><small>not to scale</small></div><ol class=\"wrows\"><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Suspected abuse</b><span class=\"win\">Under 2 years: skeletal survey with any fracture</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:14%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:38%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:56%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar inf\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:26%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Birth</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:14%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">2</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:38%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:56%\">5</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">11 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div><p class=\"wl\"><b>Older</b>a survey when suspicion is high</p></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Radial head subluxation</b><span class=\"win\">Under 5; most often 2 to 3</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:14%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:38%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:56%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:14%;--b:56%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Birth</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:14%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">2</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:38%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:56%\">5</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">11 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Supracondylar humerus fracture</b><span class=\"win\">3 to 11 years</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:14%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:38%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:56%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:38%;--b:95%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Birth</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:14%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">2</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:38%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:56%\">5</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">11 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div><p class=\"wl\"><b>Rank</b>the most common elbow fracture in children</p></div></li></ol><div class=\"lc-key\"><span><i class=\"k-inf\"></i>Report and survey</span><span><i class=\"k-ac\"></i>Reduce</span><span><i class=\"k-chr\"></i>Radiograph first</span></div></figure>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"na-s4\"><b class=\"sp-n\">4</b>Workup</h5>\n<figure class=\"lcp w5a\"><p class=\"kick\">Diagnostic workup</p><p class=\"w5z\">First step</p><div class=\"w5t first\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Every young child who will not use an arm</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">History: a pull or a fall, and whether it fits</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Lifted or pulled by the hand</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Radial head subluxation</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Fall on an outstretched hand</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Supracondylar fracture</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">No mechanism, or one that does not fit</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Suspect abuse</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Elbow exam: swelling, deformity, bony tenderness; pulses and hand nerves</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Forearm pronated; no swelling, deformity or bony tenderness</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Reduce: hyperpronation first</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Swelling, deformity or point tenderness</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Radiographs before any maneuver</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Pale or pulseless hand</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Urgent orthopedics</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Targeted tests</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">After reduction</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Watch the child use the arm for about 30 minutes</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Reaches and uses the arm</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Done: no radiograph or sling</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Still unused at about 30 minutes</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Second reduction attempt</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Unused after a second attempt, or a fall with a swollen elbow</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Anteroposterior and lateral elbow radiographs</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Posterior fat pad, or a large anterior one (sail sign)</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Occult fracture: splint, orthopedic follow-up</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Displaced supracondylar fracture</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Splint as it lies; orthopedics</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Normal film, arm still unused</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Splint and close follow-up</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Next step</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">A fracture before walking, or a story that does not fit</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Report to child protective services</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Abuse suspected</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Child protective services; safety first</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Skeletal survey under 2 years</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Occult fractures of different ages</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Abuse workup continues in its own brief</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5skip\"><span class=\"w5z\">Skip</span><span class=\"mask\">A radiograph before reducing a classic pull with a normal exam, and films after a reduction the child confirms by using the arm. Reducing a swollen elbow after a fall, or a displaced supracondylar fracture in the emergency department.</span></p></figure>\n<figure class=\"lcw\"><div class=\"ttl\"><span>After reduction</span><small>not to scale</small></div><ol class=\"wrows\"><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Uses the arm</b><span class=\"win\">Usually 10 to 20 minutes; nearly all by about 30</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:46%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:66%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:66%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Reduction</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">10 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:46%\">20 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:66%\">30 minutes</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">24 hours</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Unused at about 30 minutes</b><span class=\"win\">Second attempt; radiograph before discharge, or recheck the next day</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:46%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:66%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:66%;--b:95%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Reduction</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">10 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:46%\">20 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:66%\">30 minutes</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">24 hours</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Pain or disuse past 24 hours</b><span class=\"win\">Incomplete reduction or an occult fracture</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:26%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:46%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:66%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar inf\" style=\"--a:95%;--b:100%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Reduction</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:26%\">10 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:46%\">20 minutes</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:66%\">30 minutes</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">24 hours</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li></ol><div class=\"lc-key\"><span><i class=\"k-ac\"></i>Expected</span><span><i class=\"k-chr\"></i>Reassess</span><span><i class=\"k-inf\"></i>Suspect a fracture</span></div></figure>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Treatment by cause</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Radial head subluxation</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Hyperpronation (more first-attempt success) or supination with flexion; no immobilization; teach lifting under the arms, not by the hand; recurs in 27 to 46%</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Supracondylar fracture</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Nondisplaced or occult: splint, orthopedic follow-up. Displaced: splint as it lies, early orthopedics, often open reduction and internal fixation; most are admitted for neurovascular checks</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Compartment syndrome</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Fasciotomy without delay</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Suspected abuse<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button></div></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Report to child protective services; keep the child safe, admitting if needed</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Clues that change the answer</caption><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Points to</th><th>Next move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wrist drop, numb back of the hand</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Radial nerve at the spiral groove: midshaft humerus fracture</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Radiograph the injured area</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Cannot oppose the thumb; index and middle fingers stay straight in a fist</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Median nerve: anteromedial supracondylar fracture (anterolateral displacement typically injures the radial nerve)</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Check the radial pulse; orthopedics</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Older child usually points to the back of the upper forearm</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Possible buckle fracture of the proximal radius</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Radiograph before any maneuver</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>A second pulled elbow with the same fitting story; well child, no fracture</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Recurrence (27 to 46%)</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Reduce again; teach lifting under the arms; a skeletal survey is for a fracture or a story that does not fit</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Spiral humerus fracture in an infant not yet crawling</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Abuse<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Report; skeletal survey</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Repeated fractures with minimal trauma and blue sclerae</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Osteogenesis imperfecta</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">White sclerae and a story that does not fit point back to abuse</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"sp-pause\"><b>Pause point.</b> The bait: a radiograph before reducing a classic pull, or a skeletal survey for a recurrence with a fitting story; and the reverse, a maneuver on a swollen elbow after a fall. A pull with a normal exam earns the reduction; a fall or swelling earns the radiograph first.</p>\n\n<h5 class=\"authored-hdr\" id=\"nm-bank\">Practice</h5>\n<ol class=\"bank authored\">\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Supracondylar fracture\" data-d2=\"Clavicle fracture\" data-item-id=\"q_24718170482e5e118f19\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_459a7747ade756a6b64a\" data-d1-id=\"o_5b8882eda6235986ab82\" data-d2-id=\"o_b5cf1d3f2cab57ccb616\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Pulled up by the wrist under 5; pronated, no swelling\" data-d1-why=\"Needs a fall on the hand and a swollen, tender elbow\" data-d2-why=\"No fall, and no tenderness over the clavicle\"><span class=\"sl\">3-year-old girl who will not use her left arm</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pulled up by the wrist as she stumbled</span>\n<span class=\"si\">holds the arm extended and pronated at her side</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">No swelling, bruising or deformity; no bony point tenderness</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile</span> &rarr; <b>Radial head subluxation</b> &rarr; a pull on the arm under age 5, forearm held pronated, with no swelling, deformity or point tenderness: a clinical diagnosis; reduce without imaging</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Elbow radiographs\" data-d2=\"Sling immobilization for one week\" data-item-id=\"q_fb622f5f0dc05f8aa6c3\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_dd65283f59d1510e9b6c\" data-d1-id=\"o_b539d28827e3555baa0b\" data-d2-id=\"o_f6743d679cf4552ca968\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"She uses the arm after the maneuver: reduced\" data-d1-why=\"No films after a reduction she confirms by use\" data-d2-why=\"No immobilization after a successful reduction\"><span class=\"sl\">3-year-old girl not using her right arm after being swung by her hands</span>\n<span class=\"si\">no swelling, deformity or point tenderness</span>\n<span class=\"si\">her elbow was supinated and flexed</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">15 minutes later she reaches for toys with that arm</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile</span> &rarr; <b>No further intervention</b> &rarr; the maneuver was the treatment, and using the arm confirms the reduction: no radiograph or sling; counsel against pulling the arm</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Radial head subluxation\" data-d2=\"Radial head fracture\" data-item-id=\"q_a187fb9da9815bdda106\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_be358fc94f6d5f7eaf07\" data-d1-id=\"o_06e4c19c595052cca127\" data-d2-id=\"o_8d3a39427d96655da157\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Fall on the hand; swollen elbow; posterior fat pad\" data-d1-why=\"Follows a pull, with no swelling or tenderness\" data-d2-why=\"Tender above the joint; supracondylar is most common\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy who fell off monkey bars onto his outstretched hand</span>\n<span class=\"si\">elbow swollen and tender above the joint</span>\n<span class=\"si\">will not bend or straighten it</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Fingers warm; radial pulse present</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; lateral radiograph: posterior fat pad sign</span> &rarr; <b>Supracondylar fracture</b> &rarr; a fall on an outstretched hand with elbow swelling and a posterior fat pad: supracondylar fracture; check the median nerve and the brachial artery</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Median nerve injury\" data-d2=\"Forearm compartment syndrome\" data-item-id=\"q_2a3ffd6515f3560095ae\" data-item-version=\"3\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_6187e33f9b96512298ca\" data-d1-id=\"o_d2f0637f726ac17e0106\" data-d2-id=\"o_89236bed56865c69ae6f\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Pale, cool hand with no pulse: the brachial artery\" data-d1-why=\"Thumb opposes and the fist closes: median nerve works\" data-d2-why=\"Compartments are soft; that needs a tense forearm\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy with a displaced supracondylar fracture of the right humerus</span>\n<span class=\"si\">right hand cool and pale</span>\n<span class=\"si\">no palpable radial pulse</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Opposes the thumb and makes a full fist; forearm compartments soft</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37.0 °C &middot; capillary refill 5 seconds</span> &rarr; <b>Brachial artery injury</b> &rarr; a pale, cool, pulseless hand after a supracondylar fracture is brachial artery injury: urgent orthopedics; thumb opposition and a full fist show the median nerve works, and soft compartments argue against compartment syndrome</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Order a skeletal survey\" data-d2=\"Refer for surgical stabilization of the elbow\" data-item-id=\"q_6439d0e8c27422ee6bc7\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_b1e3f12f0193cd9b1072\" data-d1-id=\"o_f150de73155f4e7a52e1\" data-d2-id=\"o_68bf5b9f476b0402ec43\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Recurrence is common (27 to 46%); the story fits\" data-d1-why=\"For a fracture under 2, or a story that does not fit\" data-d2-why=\"Each episode is treated by reduction, not surgery\"><span class=\"sl\">3-year-old girl with her second pulled elbow this year, reduced today</span>\n<span class=\"si\">both times her brother lifted her by one hand</span>\n<span class=\"si\">using the arm normally within minutes</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Growth and development normal; no bruises or other injuries</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile</span> &rarr; <b>Counsel the family to avoid pulling the arm</b> &rarr; recurrence is common (27 to 46%); a fitting mechanism in a well child with no fracture needs counseling (lift under the arms), not an abuse workup</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Radial head subluxation\" data-d2=\"Osteogenesis imperfecta\" data-item-id=\"q_5ca49edd96a950829343\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_85bf81d0b9445f489fd5\" data-d1-id=\"o_3c126b068a695f27b3f6\" data-d2-id=\"o_6f7c9755ee7b3d55d6ac\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Spiral humerus fracture before crawling; story unfit\" data-d1-why=\"Needs a pull and no fracture; this is a fracture\" data-d2-why=\"Needs blue sclerae or repeated fractures; neither\"><span class=\"sl\">10-month-old boy who will not move his left arm</span>\n<span class=\"si\">mother says he rolled off the sofa yesterday</span>\n<span class=\"si\">not yet crawling; no prior fractures</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Bruise on the left upper arm; white sclerae</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; radiograph: spiral fracture of the humeral shaft</span> &rarr; <b>Non-accidental trauma</b> &rarr; a spiral fracture in a child who cannot generate the twist, a delay in care and a history that does not fit: suspected abuse; skeletal survey (under 2 years) and report; white sclerae make osteogenesis imperfecta less likely</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Discharge with reassurance\" data-d2=\"MRI of the elbow\" data-item-id=\"q_64b61b0020de46f9f1c4\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_50d243df41723a59fd11\" data-d1-id=\"o_a2606534adfc44955935\" data-d2-id=\"o_cde7f0385c7a171fe470\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\" data-key-why=\"Unused after two attempts: look for an occult fracture\" data-d1-why=\"Not while the arm stays unused after two attempts\" data-d2-why=\"Plain elbow radiographs come first\"><span class=\"sl\">2-year-old girl not using her arm after her mother pulled her from a puddle</span>\n<span class=\"si\">arm held extended and pronated; no swelling</span>\n<span class=\"si\">two reduction attempts, no click</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Still not reaching 30 minutes later</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no bony point tenderness</span> &rarr; <b>Radiographs of the elbow</b> &rarr; nearly all children use the arm within about 30 minutes; still unused after two attempts: elbow radiographs for an occult fracture, and if normal, splint with close follow-up</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"pearls\"><span class=\"lbl\">Pairs with</span> <i>Suspected Child Abuse</i>: corner, rib and multiple healing fractures. <i>Brachial Plexus Injury at Birth</i>: the newborn who does not move an arm.</div>\n\n<div class=\"rule\"><span class=\"lbl\">Transferable rule</span><p>Read the exam section for the treatment already given, and image only when the mechanism or the elbow says fracture.</p></div>\n\n</div>",
